course: reflection
lesson: 13
title: Hard things at home: broken promises, silence, money and illness
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some hard things at home are not one big bang but a slow drip: a dad who keeps not turning up, a dinner table nobody talks at, a fridge that is empty by Thursday, a mum always on her phone.
explain: Jayden's dad promised to come to the football final and didn't turn up again, no call; Jayden scanned the sideline the whole match, played badly and angry, and that night thought, "He never comes. Maybe I'm just not worth the drive."
explain: All week he said loudly, "I don't care if he comes," which was a shield over a hurt; a parent who keeps breaking promises is struggling with his own problems, like work, money or feelings, and it is not a measure of what the child is worth.
explain: What helped was Mum and his coach cheering loud, his nan writing every goal in a notebook "for when Dad's ready to read it", and finally telling Dad calmly on the phone, "It hurts when you don't come"; pretending not to care only kept the hurt hidden.
explain: Many children try to fix the mood at home by being the clown, the carer or the perfect one, but that is not a child's job; telling another trusted adult, like an aunt or a teacher, is how help usually starts.
fact: A parent who keeps breaking promises is usually dealing with his own problems; it does not measure how much the child is worth.
fact: Saying "I don't care" or "I'm not bothered" is often a shield over a hurt; saying calmly "it hurts when you don't come" is more honest.
fact: A silent house, with "the silent treatment" and tension, can be as stressful as shouting; children often try to fix the mood, but it is not their job.
fact: Liking a parent's new partner does not mean loving the other parent less; hearts can hold more than one person.
fact: When an older relative becomes very forgetful because of an illness, the repeating is the illness, not the person being difficult; feeling annoyed and guilty at the same time is normal.
fact: A young carer is a child who helps look after a family member; brothers and sisters of children who need lots of help often feel invisible, and their feelings matter too.
fact: Parents may argue about how to help a child, for example after a bad school report, but the argument is not the child's fault.
fact: Many families struggle to afford enough food; it is nothing to be ashamed of, and schools (breakfast clubs, free school meals) and food banks help.
fact: A food bank is a place that gives free food to families who need it.
fact: Parents who work long hours or night shifts are often doing it for the family; missing them shows how much you love them.
fact: Words said in anger, like being called useless, can stick, but they do not make it true; if shouting at home is frequent and scary, tell a trusted adult.
fact: Phones can steal attention without people noticing; saying it out loud, for example asking for phone-free dinners, often helps.
exercise: Jayden's dad missed the final again with no call. Jayden thinks he's "not worth the drive". What is true? | His dad's broken promises come from his own problems, not Jayden's worth | Jayden must have done something to make his dad stay away | Dads only come to matches when their sons win | Jayden should stop playing so there is nothing to miss
exercise: Jayden kept telling everyone "I don't care if he comes." What was really going on? | It was a shield over a real hurt | He truly didn't care at all | He was trying to make his dad angry | He had forgotten his dad promised
exercise: What helped Jayden most? | Telling Dad calmly that it hurts, and Nan's goal notebook | Pretending loudly to everyone at the club that he didn't care | Playing angrily in every match just to punish his dad for it | Never telling his dad about any of his football matches ever again
exercise: At Aisha's house dinner is silent and her parents sleep in separate rooms. She tells jokes every night. What is true? | Fixing the mood isn't her job; talking to a trusted adult helps | If she is funny enough every night, her parents will make up again | Silence at home is never stressful for children | She should shout to break the silence
exercise: Theo starts to like Mum's new boyfriend, then feels he's betraying Dad. What is true? | Liking him doesn't mean loving Dad less | He must choose between the boyfriend and Dad | Liking him is a betrayal of his real father | He should be rude to the boyfriend to stay loyal
exercise: What helped Theo? | Dad saying on the phone, "It's fine to like him, mate" | Being rude to the boyfriend every day so Dad would know he cared | Never sitting in the living room with the boyfriend ever again | Telling Mum she had to choose between the boyfriend and Dad
exercise: Lily snapped "You ALREADY asked!" at her forgetful gran, then felt horrible. What is true? | The repeating is the illness, and mixed feelings are normal | Gran is doing it on purpose to annoy Lily | Lily is a horrible person for ever feeling annoyed with her gran | Gran will remember if Lily snaps loudly enough
exercise: Ben's sister has a disability and everyone is always busy with her. Ben feels left out and then guilty. What is true? | His feelings matter too, and it's okay to say so | He is selfish for wanting any attention at all | He should hide his feelings so he isn't a bother | His parents love his sister more than him
exercise: What helped Ben? | A young carers' club and Saturday mornings alone with Dad | Hiding how he felt so he wouldn't be selfish | Being naughty so his parents would notice him | Spending all his time away from home
exercise: Chloe's parents argued in front of her about her bad school report. Whose fault was the row? | Not hers; they were worried about how to help her | Hers, because her bad report was what caused the row | Her teacher's, for writing a bad report | Nobody's, because parents never argue
exercise: Noah's family doesn't have enough food some weeks, and he's ashamed. What is true? | Many families struggle with food money; it's no shame | Only lazy families ever run out of food | He should hide it from everyone so nobody thinks badly of them | Schools never help with families' food
exercise: What helped Noah? | Telling his teacher and going to breakfast club | Hiding it and staying hungry | Taking food from other kids' lunches | Pretending he was on a diet
exercise: Ava's mum is a nurse on night shifts, and Ava wonders if she cares. What is true? | She's often working for the family, and missing her shows love | She works nights to get away from Ava | Mums who work nights don't really care about their children at all | Ava should be cold to her to show she's upset
exercise: Kofi's dad often shouts, "You're useless!" Kofi starts to believe it. What is true? | Angry words don't make it true; tell a trusted adult | If his dad says it that often, then it must really be true | He should shout the very same thing back at his dad, louder | It's normal in families, and nothing about it needs to change
exercise: Mia's parents are always on their phones at dinner. What helped? | Saying it out loud and agreeing phone-free dinners | Sulking quietly every evening | Throwing all of their phones in the bin before every dinner | Playing on her own phone to get even
test: Jayden's coach, a Geordie: "Howay, son, ye played canny, yer da's loss." ("Howay" means come on; "canny" means good; "da" is dad.) What is the coach really saying? | Jayden played well, and his dad missed out | Jayden played badly because his dad wasn't there | Jayden should stop playing until his dad comes | The coach is angry with Jayden for the result
test: What would happen if Aisha kept being "the family clown" every night for months? | She'd get tired and grumpy, and nothing would be fixed | Her parents would make up because of all the jokes and the laughing | She would get happier every single week | Nothing; it's her job to fix the mood
test: In a film, a boy who likes his mum's new partner is called a traitor by his dad and must pick a side. What's the truth in real life? | Liking a new partner doesn't mean loving Dad less | Kids must always pick one side in the end | Liking a new partner is a kind of betrayal | Kids should hate new partners to be loyal
test: Lily's older brother: "Gran's doing it on purpose, she's well annoying." What's the truth? | The forgetting is the illness, not Gran being difficult | He's right; she repeats things to wind them up | Gran could stop if she just tried harder | Gran only ever forgets the things she doesn't really care about
test: Ben thinks, "If I say I feel left out, I'm being selfish, 'cause she needs help." Which is the clever view? | Both can be true: she needs help, and his feelings still matter | Saying how he feels would take help away from her | He has no right to feel left out at all | His sister should get less help so he gets more
test: Chloe thinks, "They're fighting because of me, so I'll work till midnight every night." What would really help her? | Mum explaining they were worried, and a plan with the teacher | Working till midnight every night so that they never argue again | Hiding her next school report | Telling them to stop arguing about her
test: A kid at school says, "food banks are only for people who don't work, innit." Is he right? | No; lots of ordinary families use them, no shame | Yes; only people who don't have a job ever use them | Yes; nobody normal or hard-working ever needs free food | No, food banks are only for restaurants and cafés, not families
test: Ava's gran says her mum is a "keyworker" doing nights. ("Keyworker" means someone doing an important job, like a nurse.) What helped Ava feel closer? | Notes on the fridge and breakfasts on Mum's days off | Being cold to Mum when she came home | Staying awake all night to see her | Asking Mum to quit her job
test: Kofi's mate says, "my dad calls me useless too, it's just how dads talk." What's the truth? | Being called useless often isn't okay; tell an adult | It's normal, it's how dads talk, so there's nothing anyone can do | If dads say it, it's probably true | Kids should call their dads useless back
test: Mia's uncle, Gen X: "Back in my day we had dinner with the telly on, no biggie." What makes Mia's case worth speaking up about? | She feels ignored; saying so led to phone-free dinners | Phones are banned at all dinners by law | Telly is fine at dinner, but phones are always evil and rude | It isn't; she should just sulk instead
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a child's assertion of indifference concerning a parent's repeated absence? | It commonly serves as a shield concealing genuine hurt | It invariably signifies a genuine absence of feeling | It constitutes an attempt to provoke the absent parent | It indicates that the child has forgotten the parent's promise
exam: It is given that a child forms an attachment to a parent's new partner. Determine the response consistent with the established facts. | Such attachment does not diminish the child's love for the other parent | Such attachment constitutes a betrayal of the other parent | Such attachment obliges the child to select one parent exclusively | Such attachment is prohibited until the other parent has given his express consent
exam: Identify the statement consistent with the established facts concerning families unable to afford sufficient food. | Such hardship is common, not shameful, and schools and food banks assist | Such hardship arises solely from the idleness of the family | Such hardship must be concealed from all teachers | Such hardship is unknown among ordinary families
exam: Ascertain which of the following most accurately describes the significance of words spoken repeatedly in anger by a parent. | Such words may persist in memory but do not establish their own truth | Such words constitute an accurate assessment of the child's character | Such words are of no consequence and never affect a child | Such words should be repeated by the child to the parent in return
